  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "What is the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3}{4x-1}-\\frac{6}{5}\\,?$\n\nThe range is\n$f(x)\\in (-\\infty \\,,$\n{{field-1}}$)\u22c3($\n{{field-2}}\n$,$\n{{field-3}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=-\\frac{6}{5}.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-\\frac{6}{5},$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{6}{5})\u22c3(-\\frac{6}{5},\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078796/assets/f8a26576c0e5d3e64145052dbf58307e501b9b18f2076ff2a6808870bcbd9e39.png)",
